<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><img class="phero-img" src="/assets/img/photos/calculator-laptop.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/calculator-laptop-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/calculator-laptop.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">›</span><span aria-current="page">Appraisal word list</span></nav><p class="tag">Reference</p><h1>Insurance Claim Word List: Appraisal Terms Defined</h1><p class="lede">41 terms you’ll see in policies, estimates and appraisal awards, defined in plain English. Type to filter.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><div class="filter"><label for="gloss-filter">Search the word list</label><input id="gloss-filter" type="search" placeholder="Try “holdback” or “umpire”" autocomplete="off"><p class="small" data-gloss-count aria-live="polite">41 terms</p></div><nav class="az" aria-label="Jump to letter"><a href="#g-A">A</a><a href="#g-B">B</a><a href="#g-C">C</a><a href="#g-D">D</a><a href="#g-E">E</a><a href="#g-F">F</a><a href="#g-H">H</a><a href="#g-I">I</a><a href="#g-L">L</a><a href="#g-M">M</a><a href="#g-N">N</a><a href="#g-O">O</a><a href="#g-P">P</a><a href="#g-R">R</a><a href="#g-S">S</a><a href="#g-U">U</a><a href="#g-W">W</a><a href="#g-X">X</a></nav><dl class="glossary"><div class="gterm" id="g-A"><dt>Actual cash value (ACV)</dt><dd>Replacement cost minus depreciation. Often the first payment on a replacement cost policy.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Additional living expense (ALE)</dt><dd>Extra costs of living elsewhere while a home is uninhabitable after a covered loss.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Amount of loss</dt><dd>The dollar value of a covered loss. The only question an appraisal panel decides.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Appraisal clause</dt><dd>The policy condition that lets either side demand appraisal when they disagree on the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Appraisal award</dt><dd>The written decision signed by two of the three panel members that sets the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Appraiser</dt><dd>The person each side selects to value the loss. Policies often require that they be competent and impartial.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-B"><dt>Bad faith</dt><dd>A legal claim that an insurer unreasonably handled or denied a claim. Not decided in appraisal.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-C"><dt>Coinsurance</dt><dd>A commercial policy clause that can reduce payment if a building is insured for less than a set share of its value.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Collapse</dt><dd>A named coverage, often limited to specific causes such as the weight of ice or snow.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Cosmetic damage exclusion</dt><dd>Policy language, common on metal roofs, that excludes dents that do not affect function.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Coverage A</dt><dd>Dwelling coverage on a homeowners policy. Percentage deductibles are often based on this limit.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Coverage B</dt><dd>Other structures coverage, such as detached garages, sheds and fences.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-D"><dt>Declarations page</dt><dd>The summary page of a policy listing coverages, limits, deductibles and endorsements.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Depreciation</dt><dd>The reduction in value due to age, wear and obsolescence.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Derecho</dt><dd>A widespread, long-lived windstorm with straight-line winds, often causing damage across hundreds of miles.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Drip edge</dt><dd>Metal flashing along roof edges. Often required by code when a roof is replaced.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-E"><dt>Endorsement</dt><dd>A form that adds, removes or changes coverage in a policy.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-F"><dt>Functional damage</dt><dd>Damage that affects how a material performs, such as a fractured shingle mat.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-H"><dt>Holdback</dt><dd>Depreciation withheld until repairs are complete. Also called recoverable depreciation.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-I"><dt>Ice and water shield</dt><dd>A self-adhering underlayment used at eaves and valleys to resist ice dam leaks.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Ice dam</dt><dd>A ridge of ice at the roof edge that traps meltwater, which can back up under shingles.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-L"><dt>Line item</dt><dd>A single entry in an estimate for a specific task, quantity and price.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-M"><dt>Matching</dt><dd>Whether repaired areas can reasonably match undamaged areas in color and appearance.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Mitigation</dt><dd>Steps taken to prevent further damage after a loss, such as tarping or drying.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-N"><dt>Nebraska Department of Insurance</dt><dd>The state agency that regulates insurers and accepts consumer complaints.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-O"><dt>Ordinance or law coverage</dt><dd>Coverage for added costs to meet current building codes during repair.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Overhead and profit (O&amp;P)</dt><dd>A general contractor’s markup, often 10% and 10%, when multiple trades are involved.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-P"><dt>Percentage deductible</dt><dd>A deductible equal to a percentage of a coverage limit rather than a flat dollar amount.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Pitch</dt><dd>Roof slope, expressed as inches of rise per 12 inches of run, such as 6/12.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Proof of loss</dt><dd>A sworn statement of the amount claimed, often required within a set time after the insurer requests it.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-R"><dt>Recoverable depreciation</dt><dd>Depreciation that can be paid once repairs are completed under a replacement cost policy.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Replacement cost value (RCV)</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace with materials of like kind and quality, without depreciation.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-S"><dt>Scope of loss</dt><dd>The list of damaged items and repairs included in an estimate.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Soft metals</dt><dd>Vents, flashing, gutters and similar components that show hail dents clearly.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Square</dt><dd>100 square feet of roof surface.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Steep charge</dt><dd>Extra labor added for working on roofs above a certain pitch.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Supplement</dt><dd>A request to add missed or underpriced items to an estimate.</dd></div><div class="gterm"><dt>Suit limitation</dt><dd>The policy period within which a lawsuit against the insurer must be filed.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-U"><dt>Umpire</dt><dd>The neutral third panel member who resolves items the two appraisers cannot agree on.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-W"><dt>Wind/hail deductible</dt><dd>A separate deductible for windstorm and hail losses, often a percentage of Coverage A.</dd></div><div class="gterm" id="g-X"><dt>Xactimate</dt><dd>Estimating software widely used by insurers and contractors to price property repairs.</dd></div></dl></article>
